{
  "outcome": "needs_user_info",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi James, sorry about this, especially with your shift starting soon. Our system shows your warehouse office desktop is offline, so it isn't reaching the network. Please try these steps, in order: 1) Check that the network cable is firmly plugged into the back of the desktop and into the wall or floor port. A small light next to the port on the PC should come on. If you have a spare cable or a second wall port, try that. 2) Restart the desktop fully (Start > Power > Restart). 3) If it uses Wi-Fi, turn Wi-Fi off and back on, or forget the network and reconnect. After that, please reply with two things: whether the problem is fixed, and whether other people or devices in the warehouse office have lost network too. If several people are affected, it's probably the office network and not your PC, and we'll pass it straight to our network team. If you're still stuck before your shift, reply and we'll move it up right away.",
  "internal_note": "Sender: James O'Connor (user_id 8), warehouse supervisor, account active, nothing unusual. Assigned device BL-DT-0108 (Windows 11 desktop) shows offline, with last check-in at 2026-10-09T12:22:42Z. No warnings, and the disk is healthy (78% free). It's not yet clear whether only this device is affected or the whole warehouse office network is down. Gave the user steps from KB-014 and KB-018: check the cable and port, restart, and reconnect Wi-Fi if it uses it. Asked whether colleagues are affected. Next: if several users are down, escalate as a network outage (P1 or P2 depending on how many are blocked) per KB-014. If the device comes online, re-run check_device_status to confirm a fresh check-in. If it's still offline after the cable and restart steps while others work, escalate to Tier 2 or on-site for a NIC, cable, or switch port fault. The user's shift starts in about an hour.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-014",
    "KB-018"
  ],
  "confidence": "medium"
}
